in the context of something like LaTeX (which defines a Language just =
like C)
additions means compromising the exchangeability of documents between
different implementations. I could bet that even with the few peoples on =
this
list we will find more than 3 different releases of LaTeX being the =
production
one at their site/computer. Nevertheless we expect that essentially any =
LaTeX
document those source is being send to the list would be processable by
everybody (and unless you trying deliberately to build on fixed bugs etc =
it
will be true). That is not at all true if you keep adding features which =
will
get added into the kernel or package macros so that only those people =
with the
latest release will be able to process stuff.

frank

